<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ross, Scotland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ross is a region of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Highlands of Scotland</a> stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moray-firth">Moray Firth</a> to the west coast. It became an earldom in the early 13th century, and the claim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donald-lord-of-the-isles">Donald, Lord of the Isles</a>, to the earldom led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-harlaw">Battle of Harlaw</a> in 1411. The Lords of the Isles held the earldom from 1437 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-islay-earl-of-ross">John of Islay</a> forfeited it to the crown in 1475. It later formed the county of Ross and Cromarty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ross-scotland.jpg" alt="Ross, Scotland" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benson85 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ross_(district).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ross is divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wester-ross">Wester Ross</a>, sparsely peopled, mountainous and cut by sea lochs opening onto the Minch, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-ross">Easter Ross</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moray-firth">Moray Firth</a>, where the land is more level and fertile. Its main towns, Dingwall, Cromarty, Fortrose, Invergordon and Tain, all lie on the east coast, Cromarty and Fortrose on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-isle">Black Isle</a> peninsula. The name may come from a Gaelic word for headland, perhaps meaning the Black Isle, or from Hrossey, the West Norse name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> hunter-gatherers lived on the coast at Sand, Applecross, and Ptolemy's Geography of the 2nd century places a tribe called the Decantae in what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-ross">Easter Ross</a>. Later the land was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the 6th and 7th centuries by followers of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> and raided for three centuries by Norwegian Viking raiders, whose presence survives in place-names such as Dingwall and Tain. Sculptured cross-slabs said by tradition to mark the graves of three sons of a Danish king shipwrecked off Nigg stand at Shandwick and Nigg; the third, from Hilton of Cadboll, was moved to the grounds of Invergordon Castle.</p><p>Ross is first recorded as a territory in a life of Saint Cathróe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, written soon after his death between 971 and 976, and seems to have been separated from the Province of Moray in the 10th century, the boundary following the River Beauly. The Scottish crown claimed overlordship of Ross from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1098, but effective royal authority took many years to establish. The Diocese of Ross existed by the mid-12th century and was based at Fortrose Cathedral by the 13th.</p><p>By the 16th century the land was divided among clans: the Rosses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-ross">Easter Ross</a>, the Munros around Ben Wyvis and Dingwall, the Macleods, the Macdonalds of Glengarry and the Mackenzies. Ross was finally separated from Inverness-shire as a shire in 1661. Apart from clan feuds, the only battles fought in it were at Invercarron, where Colonel Archibald Strachan crushed Montrose on 27 April 1650, and at Glenshiel, where Jacobites aided by Spaniards were defeated on 10 June 1719.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ross is now part of the Highland council area, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> still keeps its name in the Presbytery of Ross. The Shandwick cross, broken in a storm in 1847 and re-erected, is preserved in a glass pavilion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ross, Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its location is not stated, and historians have suggested Moray, <b>Ross</b> or the area around the Moray Firth.</blockquote><a href="/state/fidach">Fidach</a></li><li><blockquote>Donald of the Isles fought the Battle of Harlaw in 1411 over the earldom of <b>Ross</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-the-isles">Lordship of the Isles</a></li></ul></section>
